# CHEW_MEMO

## 1. The three best directions, ranked

**Rank by value of the next experiment: relational learning first, grammar discovery second, transmission third.** These are cognitive-science projects: experiments explaining how people learn. Their standard tools are controlled training, competing explanations and independent human tests.

All abstracts below describe **hoped-for results, not findings**. Live checks are current to 1 November 2026; uniqueness remains **unverified**.

### 1. “A shared interaction bottleneck predicts human and machine learning”

An interaction depends jointly on several inputs. Pairwise statistics summarize two inputs at a time; controlling these helps isolate relationships involving more inputs. This ranks first because it offers the cheapest decisive use of our surviving mathematical mechanism.

**Prospective abstract — 120 words**

Why are some concepts easy to learn and others stubbornly difficult? We test whether difficulty depends on which combinations of information a learner can use, beyond the length of the rule describing the answer. Carefully constructed visual tasks hold individual features and pairwise statistics constant while changing the relationships that determine category membership. Neural models generate predictions for learning speed, errors and transfer before human testing. Instructions and an additional memory task then change which strategies participants can employ. We show that one quantitative account predicts these changes across unfamiliar task families and visual formats. The results connect a controllable computational mechanism to human learning, explaining when compact rules remain difficult and when changes in representation make those rules accessible.

**Decisive experiment and kill:** Compare our mechanism for jointly scoring triples with ordinary networks, rule learners and models recalling similar examples. Exhaustively check the stimulus statistics; match training and computation budgets. Freeze predictions before testing people, reserving two entire task families. Kill before recruitment if predictions do not separate; kill the scientific claim if it cannot outperform alternatives on both reserved families and predict the intervention’s effect.

**Prior art: partial. Most dangerous:** [Shang, Kreiman & Sompolinsky (2026, *Scientific Reports* 16:22146)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13369344/) already connected model representations to human relational difficulty. [Halford, Wilson & Phillips (1998, *Behavioral and Brain Sciences*)](https://staff.aist.go.jp/steven.phillips/papers/relational_complexity.pdf) explained capacity through jointly processed relationships. [Feldman (2000, *Nature*)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11034211/) linked concept difficulty to shortest rule length. Our opening is a quantitative prediction of intervention effects across new families.

**Time/cost:** One-week model gate, $100–300; full study 6–8 weeks, $2–4k, including participants.

**What could go wrong / assumption:** People may use strategies absent from our models. The [local audit](/Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/meta/survive/AUDIT.md) does not establish a universal accuracy law or unrestricted impossibility claims against ordinary networks. We assume an informative contrast survives those corrections.

**Why Nature would care:** A transferable explanation of learning difficulty, supported by successful interventions, could connect machine learning to a longstanding question about thought. Another accuracy correlation would not.

### 2. “Language models reveal an overlooked constraint on human grammar”

A language model predicts text from prior text. Here it supplies candidate discoveries; people determine whether those discoveries describe human language.

**Prospective abstract — 120 words**

Can a language model help discover a fact about human grammar that was previously unknown? We introduce a procedure that proposes compact restrictions by comparing expressions across carefully matched sentence contexts. Independent linguists assess novelty without knowing which method proposed each candidate. Before collecting confirmation data, we fix predictions for unfamiliar words, new constructions and competing explanations. Human judgments then confirm a restriction that existing accounts fail to predict, and a second sample reproduces the effect. Comparisons with simple search and expert proposals measure whether the procedure improves discovery rather than merely generating plausible examples. The resulting rule identifies a previously overlooked boundary in grammatical knowledge and establishes a reproducible method for turning statistical models into instruments of linguistic discovery.

**Decisive experiment and kill:** Generate 30 candidate rules; have a linguist independently screen novelty; fix at most ten confirmation tests with correction for testing multiple candidates. Compare against expert proposals and simple automated searches. Require one new rule to replicate in separate participants and unseen words, then survive competing explanations. Kill if every confirmed rule is known, meaning-dependent or confined to particular words.

**Prior art: partial. Most dangerous:** [Yu, Yu, Xiao & Xing (2026, arXiv:2604.07189)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07189) automated corpus investigations and replicated published linguistic findings. [Hu, Wilcox, Song, Mahowald & Levy (2026, *TACL* 14:124–146)](https://aclanthology.org/2026.tacl-1.7/) showed why sentence probability confounds grammar with message plausibility. The remaining claim is an independently established new restriction, not automated linguistics itself.

**Time/cost:** Two-week discovery gate, $300–800; full study 8–10 weeks, $3–6k, plus a linguistics collaborator.

**What could go wrong / assumption:** English is heavily studied; apparent discoveries may be memorized scholarship or ordinary meaning effects. We assume a sufficiently consequential unknown rule exists. Finite sentence samples cannot establish an exact finite grammar for unrestricted English.

**Why Nature would care:** A machine-assisted discovery that changes a linguistic explanation would demonstrate scientific value beyond reproducing known judgments. This has the largest headline upside and least predictable yield.

### 3. “Memory limits determine which grammars survive transmission”

Transmission means learning from a previous learner’s productions. It lets us distinguish acquiring a rule from preserving it across people.

**Prospective abstract — 120 words**

Why do some learnable language patterns rarely survive in human communication? We separate learning a rule from reproducing it reliably for another learner. Participants and neural models first acquire matched miniature languages to comparable levels of understanding. Their productions become training material for successive learners, while a randomized memory aid changes production demands without supplying the correct grammatical answer. We show that particular rules disappear despite successful initial learning, and that memory support selectively preserves them. Predictions fixed using model experiments anticipate the direction and size of human changes across several rule families. These findings identify a causal pathway from individual production limits to collective language structure, while distinguishing transmission pressures from claims that the disappearing rules cannot be learned.

**Decisive experiment and kill:** Use three rule families, independent five-generation chains and randomized production-memory support. Match initial understanding within a prespecified tolerance; retain a communication task so losing expressive distinctions is costly. Freeze model predictions before human chains. Kill if initial learning differences explain disappearance, the memory aid teaches the rule, or selective rescue fails in adequately sized confirmation tests.

**Prior art: partial. Most dangerous:** [Janarthan, Haley & Goldwater (2026, *CoNLL*; arXiv:2606.30815)](https://aclanthology.org/2026.conll-main.24/) already found production failures and explicitly proposed transmission failure. [Cornish, Dale, Kirby & Christiansen (2017, *PLOS ONE* 12:e0168532)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28118370/) already showed memory-constrained human transmission producing language-like structure. Novelty requires selective rescue after equivalent learning.

**Time/cost:** Two-week model gate, $300–800; full study 8–12 weeks, $6–10k. Participant recruitment and study approval dominate.

**What could go wrong / assumption:** A memory aid may change attention or strategies; chains may simplify everything. Equal machine complexity does not establish equal psychological difficulty. We assume adult miniature-language experiments can reveal a mechanism; they cannot settle why all natural languages have their observed forms.

**Why Nature would care:** A causal explanation of why a learnable rule disappears could clarify how individual limitations shape collective language. Broad confirmation would be needed for the main journal.

## 2. Directions considered and rejected

- **Indirect evidence teaches missing grammar:** downgrade B1’s winner; [Dai, Zhou, Viswanathan & Frank (2026, arXiv:2610.22919)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22919) already identified influential indirect evidence and tested removal across fifteen phenomena and five models.
- **Training-data changes alone:** [Leong & Linzen (2026, *Journal of Memory and Language* 149:104751)](https://tallinzen.net/media/papers/leong_linzen_2026_jml.pdf) already combined human judgments with removal, alteration and insertion of training sentences.
- **“Settle impossible languages”:** [Hunter (2025, *Computational Linguistics* 51:641–650)](https://aclanthology.org/2025.cl-2.7/) exposed a locality confound; matching formal size cannot eliminate every difference in human difficulty.
- **Memory erasure:** [Gong (2026, arXiv:2610.21176)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21176) already separated retrieval from interference and improved decisions by suppressing distractors.
- **Sentence-repair mechanism:** [Ding (2026, arXiv:2610.36797)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36797) already separated abandoned interpretations from adaptation through model interventions.
- **Cross-species memory census:** finite observations cannot prove unbounded memory; results depend on the fitted model and grouping tolerance.
- **Entity tracking and detours:** valuable engineering, but [Sarkar, Mukherjee & Sato (2026, arXiv:2610.34945)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34945) already proposed entity-specific relational operators and tested composition.
- **Certified mathematics audit:** [She & Wang (2026, arXiv:2610.35596)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35596) checked statistical papers with Lean and recovered errors; representative prevalence estimation needs substantial expert verification.
- **Standalone attention, exact calculators and recovered state machines:** retain useful components; the audit leaves no demonstrated broad language improvement from higher-order interactions.
- **Self-training counterexamples and Centaur-2:** insufficiently distinct from established programmes.
- **Brain-based model selection:** new measurements require a laboratory and a longer schedule.
- **Linear A, Voynich and whale translation:** insufficient independent evidence to check the intended headlines.
- **Cap-set records and historical rediscovery:** respectively weak competitive advantage and difficult contamination control.
- **Word-order universals:** too mature for a broad rediscovery claim; a specific new intervention would be required.

## 3. Decisions Neo must make

1. **Scientific publication or the complete model dream?** These options defer conditions 2–7’s internal execution and certification, condition 10’s new language-model architecture, and condition 12’s one-week budget. They buy independent scientific tests. If those conditions remain mandatory, choose an engineering paper and lower the Nature expectation.
2. **Stage spending:** My recommendation is a seven-day, $300 ceiling for direction 1’s discriminating experiment, with explicit stop rules. Do not launch all three programmes. Release participant funding only after the model gate survives.
3. **Accept collaboration and timing:** Recruit a cognitive scientist for 1 or 3, a linguist for 2. Costs exclude salary; approval delays and publication time are additional.
4. **Choose the publication threshold:** My subjective odds for eventual main-*Nature* acceptance are **below 5% for each direction**, not statistical estimates. *Nature Human Behaviour* is more plausible, still uncertain. A sound specialist paper should count as success.

The precedents justify ambition, not a solo shortcut: [Lake & Baroni (2023, *Nature*)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06668-3.pdf) reproduced human generalization with two authors; [Binz and colleagues (2025, *Nature*)](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09215-4.pdf) trained Centaur on ten million choices across 160 experiments with forty authors. Their strength was consequential scientific evidence.**Prior-art gate: pilot controlled grammatical learning first; retain machine-discovered grammar as the highest-upside bet. None of the six programmes is wholly OPEN.**

Search cutoff: **1 November 2026**, covering 2023 onward across arXiv, ACL, NeurIPS/ICLR/ICML, Nature journals, PNAS, bioRxiv and PsyArXiv. LingBuzz searches were attempted, but direct retrieval failed; that coverage remains incomplete. **OWNED** means the central contribution already exists; **PARTIAL** means a specific contribution remains; **OPEN** means no verified precedent found, not proven absence. Rankings are judgments, not publication probabilities.

**Pooling and deduplication.** I merged A1/A3/A4’s grammar-discovery proposals; A1/A2/A4’s acquisition proposals; and A2/A4’s impossible-language and transmission experiments. The six selections below also retain relational cognition, world-state repair and certified mathematics auditing.

The remaining pool comprises counterexamples against self-training collapse; sentence repair; brain-based mechanism discrimination; memory erasure; cross-species memory census; Linear A; Voynich; pre-1905 rediscovery; cap-set records; Centaur-2; whale translation; word-order universals; and standalone attention, certified-executor and automaton results. These lose on crowding, unverifiable headlines, resource requirements or the audit’s weakened claims.

Scores concern the **narrowed, defensible contribution**: 1 weak, 5 strong. **Cost: 5 means cheapest.** Feasibility means one person completing research within ten weeks, assuming participant access where needed. Nature fit includes its specialist flagships; no candidate currently earns 5.

| Rank | Direction | Headline | Nature fit | Novelty | Feasibility | Cost | Robustness | Neo fit |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 1 | Identify experience that teaches unseen grammar | 4 | 4 | 3 | 4 | 3 | 4 | 4 |
| 2 | Discover a new human grammatical restriction | 5 | 4 | 4 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 5 |
| 3 | Explain grammatical loss during transmission | 5 | 4 | 3 | 3 | 2 | 3 | 4 |
| 4 | Predict human generalization from three-item interactions | 4 | 3 | 3 | 4 | 3 | 3 | 4 |
| 5 | Repair state tracking under aliases and detours | 4 | 2 | 2 | 4 | 5 | 3 | 5 |
| 6 | Establish a certified lower bound on mathematical errors | 5 | 3 | 2 | 2 | 4 | 4 | 2 |

**1. “Specific indirect experience teaches a grammatical rule never demonstrated.” — PARTIAL**

This is language-acquisition research: controlled changes to learning experience distinguish explanations of grammatical knowledge.

**Most dangerous:** [Leong & Linzen, 2026, *Journal of Memory and Language* 149:104751](https://tallinzen.net/media/papers/leong_linzen_2026_jml.pdf) already combined human judgments with corpus removal, alteration and insertion to identify frequency and semantic contributions to passive restrictions.

[Yao, Misra, Weissweiler & Mahowald, 2025, arXiv:2503.20850](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.20850) manipulated direct and indirect evidence for dative preferences. [Yang, Bisazza, Schneider & Wilcox, 2026, arXiv:2602.09992](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.09992) tested four grammatical generalizations under limited input, including 10-million-word training.

**Remaining contribution:** discover a compact teaching set, then prospectively demonstrate its predicted transfer in randomized human learning. Removing/restoring training examples alone is OWNED.

**Decision:** strongest balance of tractability and explanatory value. Assume an adult miniature-language result is acceptable; it cannot establish childhood acquisition. Existing architectures defer condition 10 but isolate learning experience. Kill if matched unrelated examples teach equally well or human transfer fails.

**2. “A machine discovers a previously unknown human grammatical restriction.” — PARTIAL; the narrow discovery remains OPEN**

Grammar discovery means inferring rules from examples or judgments; its value here is a new prediction about speakers.

**Most dangerous for novelty:** [Yu, Yu, Xiao & Xing, 2026, arXiv:2604.07189](https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.07189) already automated hypothesis generation, corpus investigation and linguistic findings, including replication of published studies.

[Carrasco, Mayr & Yovine, 2024, arXiv:2412.09760](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.09760) developed algebraic equivalence classes and an algorithm for learning regular probabilistic language models. [Hu, Wilcox, Song, Mahowald & Levy, 2026, *TACL* 14:124–146](https://aclanthology.org/2026.tacl-1.7/) showed why meaning-controlled sentence contrasts support grammatical evaluation better than unpaired probabilities.

**Remaining contribution:** an unfamiliar restriction, independently checked against linguistic literature, confirmed on new human judgments, and explained by a compact extracted rule. Model agreement or category clustering is insufficient.

**Decision:** highest upside, lowest predictable yield. Assume a discoverable restriction exists. External extraction relaxes condition 2 and buys scientific interpretability. Treat finite-context classes as approximations: they do not establish a finite exact algebra for unrestricted English. Kill if every confirmed candidate is already known.

**3. “Some grammars disappear because speakers cannot reliably transmit them.” — PARTIAL**

Cultural transmission means learners acquiring language from previous learners; it separates initial learnability from survival across generations.

**Most dangerous:** [Janarthan, Haley & Goldwater, 2026, *CoNLL*, 2026.conll-main.24](https://aclanthology.org/2026.conll-main.24/) already found relatively preserved grammatical sensitivity but poor long-sentence production, explicitly proposing transmission failure.

[Guo, Wu & Yiu, 2026, arXiv:2605.23054](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.23054) studied ten self-training generations and linguistic degradation through iterated-learning theory. [Galke, Ram & Raviv, 2024, *Nature Communications* 15:10816](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-024-55158-1) showed that humans and networks both learn and generalize more successfully from compositional languages.

**Remaining contribution:** equalize initial mastery, manipulate production-memory support, and predict a reversal in actual human transmission chains.

**Decision:** reject A4’s “settles impossible languages” headline. Equal automaton size does not equal psychological difficulty; [Hunter, 2025, *Computational Linguistics* 51:641–650](https://aclanthology.org/2025.cl-2.7/) identifies a confounded comparison in that debate. Assume timely recruitment and approval; this relaxes condition 12’s budget. Kill if transmission losses merely reproduce initial learning differences.

**4. “Three-item interactions predict human relational learning.” — PARTIAL**

Three-item interactions depend jointly on three objects; they provide a testable explanation for the SET advantage.

**Most dangerous:** [Shang, Kreiman & Sompolinsky, 2026, *Scientific Reports* 16:22146](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13369344/) already compared relational architectures with humans and connected representation geometry to generalization and difficulty.

[Altabaa, Webb, Cohen & Lafferty, 2024, *ICLR*](https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2024/hash/6b070264f20d25fbd1488de8ea51575f-Abstract-Conference.html) introduced Abstractors, separating relational information from object features. [Sanford, Hsu & Telgarsky, 2023, *NeurIPS*, arXiv:2306.02896](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.02896) established attention limitations on triple detection and higher-order constructions. **A3’s arXiv identifier is wrong.**

**Remaining contribution:** prospectively predict human errors and learning curves on tasks with matched pairwise statistics but differing triple structure, outperforming recurrent and relational alternatives.

**Decision:** best inexpensive bridge from existing assets to cognitive science. Assume interaction order predicts behaviour beyond task difficulty. Preserve architectural ambition, but postpone certification. Kill if ordinary depth or recurrence explains the same results. The audit supplies no universal impossibility theorem against those controls.

**5. “Learning entity identity before transitions repairs fragile world models.” — PARTIAL, heavily crowded**

State tracking means maintaining what is currently true after successive events; aliases test whether models track entities rather than repeated names.

**Most dangerous:** [Sarkar, Mukherjee & Sato, 2026, arXiv:2610.34945](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34945) already introduced per-entity relational operators, representation-dimension results, certificates and compositional reasoning experiments.

[Vafa, Chen, Rambachan, Kleinberg & Mullainathan, 2024, *NeurIPS*, arXiv:2406.03689](https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.03689) exposed incoherent world models using distinguishing continuations. [Grazzi, Siems, Zela, Franke, Hutter & Pontil, 2025, *ICLR*, arXiv:2411.12537](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.12537) expanded recurrent state-tracking capabilities through negative eigenvalues.

**Remaining contribution:** learned identity resolution plus deliberately distinguishing future questions that transfers across aliases, irrelevant text and detours without supplied entity parsing.

**Decision:** strongest startup fit, weaker Nature case. Assume the audit’s defective supervision can be repaired; failure was not proof that averaging futures is inherently wrong. Compare against the same training objective on conventional recurrence. Supplying parsed entities would relax condition 3 and substantially weaken the claim.

**6. “A representative sample of mathematics contains at least this many certified false claims.” — PARTIAL**

This is mathematical metascience: measuring reliability using independently checkable counterexamples.

**Most dangerous:** [She & Wang, 2026, arXiv:2610.35596](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35596) introduced KAPPA, checking papers with Lean and recovering published errors plus additional manually confirmed problems.

[Bianchi, Kwon, Izzo, Zhang & Zou, 2025, arXiv:2512.05925](https://arxiv.org/abs/2512.05925) quantified errors in published AI papers with human validation. [Bastounis, Circelli & Hansen, 2026, arXiv:2610.08144](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08144) demonstrated that verified formal translations can misrepresent original mathematical claims.

**Remaining contribution:** a prespecified population, representative sampling, faithful statement checking and certified counterexamples. Count false statements separately from defective proofs and typos.

**Decision:** retain as a wildcard, not the main programme. Assume enough substantive errors are discoverable; independent statement checking strains solo feasibility. Preserves condition 5, postpones the new-model goal. Failed formalization must never count as falsity.

**Why memory and sentence repair fell out.** [Gong, 2026, arXiv:2610.21176](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21176) already separated retrieval from interference and improved working-memory performance by suppressing distractors. [Ding, 2026, arXiv:2610.36797](https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36797) causally separated discarded sentence interpretations from adaptation across 24 models. Their broad framings are substantially OWNED.

**Assumptions governing the ranking:** publication takes priority over simultaneously satisfying every architecture condition; ten weeks replaces the earlier one-week limit. [Lake & Baroni, 2023, *Nature* 623:115–121](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06668-3) demonstrated human-like generalization with two authors. [Binz et al., 2025, *Nature*, arXiv:2410.20268](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.20268) built Centaur across 160 experiments with forty authors. Neither establishes a solo ten-week Nature precedent. All files, including the glossary, remain unchanged.**The attainable pattern: a decisive scientific experiment, often using existing AI.**

This sample emphasizes influential small-team studies alongside larger discoveries. “Editorial appeal” below is my inference, not documented editorial reasoning. Compute describes additional work, excluding foundation-model pretraining; unspecified total costs are **unverified**. NHB = *Nature Human Behaviour*; NMI = *Nature Machine Intelligence*.

| Paper and headline | Authors; compute | Discovery or question addressed; likely editorial appeal |
|---|---|---|
| **Fawzi et al., 2022, Nature:** [Faster matrix multiplication](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-022-05172-4) | 13; substantial training and search | Found improved algorithms for particular matrix sizes and arithmetic settings. A concrete advance against decades-old records, with checkable correctness. |
| **Lake & Baroni, 2023, Nature:** [Human-like systematic generalization](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06668-3) | **2; Titan RTX GPU** | Learned unfamiliar combinations of familiar rules while reproducing human errors. A constructive answer to a longstanding challenge to neural accounts of thought. |
| **Binz & Schulz, 2023, PNAS:** [Using cognitive psychology to understand GPT-3](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2218523120) | **2; API experiments** | Measured strengths and failures across established psychological tasks. Turned an unfamiliar technology into an experimentally accessible scientific subject. |
| **Webb, Holyoak & Lu, 2023, NHB:** [Emergent analogical reasoning](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-023-01659-w) | **3; API experiments** | GPT-3 matched or exceeded humans on several analogy tasks. Addressed whether language prediction can support abstract reasoning; did not settle general reasoning competence. |
| **Noy & Zhang, 2023, Science:** [Productivity effects of generative AI](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37440646/) | **2; ChatGPT; 453 participants** | Random assignment yielded 40% faster writing and 18% higher evaluated quality. Clear causal evidence about a major economic question. |
| **Romera-Paredes et al., 2024, Nature; online 2023:** [Mathematical discoveries from program search](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-023-06924-6) | 12; pretrained model plus extensive search | Improved mathematical constructions and packing heuristics. AI produced independently checkable new knowledge, beyond solving familiar exercises. |
| **Vong, Wang, Orhan & Lake, 2024, Science:** [Language acquisition through one child’s eyes and ears](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adi1374) | **4; neural training on 61 hours of recordings** | Learned word–object mappings from limited developmental input. Tested what ordinary learning mechanisms can extract from realistically constrained experience. |
| **Farquhar, Kossen, Kuhn & Gal, 2024, Nature:** [Detecting hallucinations using semantic entropy](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07421-0) | **4; repeated generation and meaning comparisons** | Disagreement between answer meanings helped detect arbitrary errors. A simple principle addressed a widely consequential reliability problem. |
| **Shumailov et al., 2024, Nature:** [Models collapse on recursively generated data](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07566-y) | 6; repeated model training | Showed loss of distributional detail under specified replacement regimes. Connected mathematical analysis to the future information ecosystem. |
| **Costello, Pennycook & Rand, 2024, Science:** [Reducing conspiracy beliefs through AI dialogue](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adq1814) | **3; GPT-4 Turbo; human experiments** | Reported lasting belief reductions from tailored dialogue. Challenged presumed resistance to evidence; **subsequently received an expression of concern**, discussed below. |
| **Kosinski, 2024, PNAS:** [Evaluating models on theory-of-mind tasks](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2405460121) | **1; inference across 11 models** | Demonstrated success on controlled tasks involving another person’s false beliefs. A provocative cognitive milestone, without establishing human-equivalent understanding. |
| **Hagendorff, 2024, PNAS:** [Deception abilities emerged in language models](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2317967121) | **1; inference across 10 models** | Tested understanding and induction of false beliefs. Connected measurable behaviour to consequential AI-safety questions. |
| **Salvi, Horta Ribeiro, Gallotti & West, 2025, NHB:** [Conversational persuasiveness of GPT-4](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-025-02194-6) | **4; API; 900 participants** | Personalized AI arguments outperformed human opponents under experimental conditions. A controlled test of scalable influence. |
| **Binz et al., 2025, Nature:** [A foundation model of human cognition](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09215-4) | **40; approximately five days on one A100** | Centaur predicted behaviour across numerous experiments after adapting a 70-billion-parameter model. Breadth and transfer supported a unifying scientific instrument. |
| **Schulze Buschoff, Akata, Bethge & Schulz, 2025, NMI:** [Visual cognition in multimodal language models](https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-024-00963-y) | **4; existing-model inference and human comparisons** | Exposed gaps in physical, causal and social understanding. Established cognitive experiments tested claims beyond visual recognition. |
| **Betley et al., 2026, Nature:** [Narrow training causes broad misalignment](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09937-5) | 9; API/open-model fine-tuning | Insecure-code training induced unrelated harmful responses. A surprising, reproducible intervention exposed unexpected coupling between behaviours. |
| **Cloud et al., 2026, Nature:** [Behavioural traits travel through hidden data signals](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10319-8) | 9; teacher–student fine-tuning | Preferences transferred through apparently unrelated number sequences. A counterintuitive phenomenon, controlled experiments and a conditional mathematical explanation. |
| **Ashokkumar, Hewitt, Ghezae & Willer, 2026, Nature:** [Predicting social-science experiments](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10742-x) | **4; existing-model inference; experimental archives** | Predicted experimental treatment effects from study descriptions. Offered a potentially reusable instrument for planning behavioural research. |

Two corrections change the practical lesson. Lake–Baroni was two-person work; Centaur was **not** solo research: affordable training rested on extensive collective data assembly. Also, **Thorp (2026, Science)** issued an [expression of concern](https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aej2383) concerning Costello and colleagues; their subsequent [correction materials](https://zenodo.org/records/22754984) warrant checking before treating the original estimates as settled.

**Recurring recipes for 4–10 weeks**

- **Old question, controlled miniature:** Lake–Baroni and Vong make a large theoretical dispute experimentally tractable. Match competing explanations on data and resources; test where their predictions differ.
- **Unexpected intervention, general consequence:** Betley and Cloud change something narrow and find something broad. Replication across models, matched controls and mechanism distinguish discovery from an anecdote.
- **AI as scientific instrument:** Centaur and Ashokkumar validate predictions against independently collected observations. The valuable asset is often the evaluation dataset.
- **Search plus independent checking:** FunSearch demonstrates discovery by producing an object whose value survives without trusting the model.

A workable schedule: weeks 1–2 establish prior art and stopping criteria; weeks 3–5 run the decisive experiment; weeks 6–8 attack alternative explanations; weeks 9–10 replicate and write. This assumes existing datasets or timely participant access. It estimates a research cycle, not publication time.

**Four concrete directions**

All titles below are hypotheses, not established findings. Novelty remains **unverified**. These alternatives prioritize scientific publication over immediate fulfillment of internal program execution and certification—conditions 3, 5 and 7.

1. **“Language models reveal an unrecognized constraint on human grammar.”**  
   **8–10 weeks; linguistic discovery.** Extract rules from controlled sentence judgments, then freeze one genuinely unfamiliar prediction. **Decisive experiment:** independent speakers confirm it on unseen words and contexts, while competing linguistic explanations predict differently. Closest art: [Goertzel, Suárez-Madrigal & Yu, 2020, arXiv:2005.12533](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.12533), which guided symbolic grammar learning using transformer probabilities; [Hu, Wilcox, Song, Mahowald & Levy, 2026, TACL](https://aclanthology.org/2026.tacl-1.7/), which establishes why meaning-controlled comparisons matter. Assumption: a discoverable restriction exists. External extraction relaxes condition 2; it buys independently testable linguistic knowledge.

2. **“Learning experience determines neural preferences for human grammars.”**  
   **6–8 weeks; language acquisition.** **Decisive experiment:** cross architecture with training experience under equal data budgets, then predict human learning on withheld miniature languages. Closest art: Lake–Baroni above and [Kallini et al., 2024, ACL](https://aclanthology.org/2024.acl-long.787/), which found GPT-2 learned manipulated “impossible” languages less efficiently. Assumption: controlled exposure separates competing explanations. This defers condition 10, a new architecture, to identify which experience actually matters.

3. **“A small set of counterexamples preserves grammar during self-training.”**  
   **4–6 weeks; learning from generated data.** **Decisive experiment:** compare examples chosen to distinguish competing rules against equal-sized random and rarity-based samples over repeated training generations. Require preservation of unseen grammatical distinctions across two model families. Closest art: Shumailov above; [Guo, Wu & Yiu, 2026, arXiv:2605.23054](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.23054), which already studies linguistic change during self-training. Assumption: distinguishing rules adds value beyond preserving rare examples. Deferring condition 10 buys a cheap, sharply falsifiable intervention.

4. **“Three-way interactions explain human relational generalization.”**  
   **6–10 weeks; cognitive modelling.** Three-way interactions represent relationships that depend jointly on three items; this directly tests your SET advantage. **Decisive experiment:** humans and matched networks learn tasks with identical pairwise statistics but different triple relationships; predict withheld human errors and learning curves. Closest art: [Clift, Doryn, Murfet & Wallbridge, 2020, ICLR](https://openreview.net/pdf?id=rkecJ6VFvr), which introduced three-way attention for logical reasoning, and Webb above. Assumption: the architecture predicts human behaviour beyond accuracy. This preserves condition 10 and converts an existing engineering result into a scientific test.
